<FigureFrame id="coaching-requirements" title="What an AI coach should support" intro="Venture work, learning, and reflection call for different kinds of support. Founders keep control of decisions.">
  <div class="coaching-bands">
    <section class="coaching-band"><h3>Task handling</h3><dl><div><dt>Keep work moving</dt><dd>Turn insights into next steps and keep track of progress.</dd></div></dl></section>
    <section class="coaching-band"><h3>Sensemaking</h3><dl class="coaching-pair">
      <div><dt>Clarify the venture</dt><dd>Build and revise a coherent account of how the venture should work.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Test assumptions</dt><dd>Make assumptions explicit, plan tests, and use evidence to revise the venture.</dd></div>
    </dl></section>
    <section class="coaching-band"><h3>Personal development</h3><dl><div><dt>Reflect on decisions</dt><dd>Use questions and constructive feedback to support founders’ judgment.</dd></div></dl></section>
  </div>
  <p class="figure-reading">Founders can accept, revise, or reject suggestions. Desk research and simulations can prepare a test; they cannot replace evidence from the outside world.</p>
  <Fragment slot="caption">Explanation based on Figure 1 of <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-032-28313-9_2">Liebschner et al. (2026)</a> (author manuscript, p. 10), with simplified labels and layout. The paper positions these needs from task support toward more specialized reflective support. These are interview-derived design requirements, not tested effects or steps in a founder’s development.</Fragment>
</FigureFrame>
